%description:
IPv4 fragment reassembly timeout: when the reassembly timer for a partially received
datagram expires, the receiver must give up on its own initiative and report the event
with ICMP Time Exceeded, code 1.

h1 sends two 3000 B UDP datagrams to h2, at t=1s and at t=100s. h1's Ethernet MTU is
500 B, so each datagram becomes 7 fragments. The r--h2 link is 100x slower than h1--r
and r's egress queue holds a single packet, so only the first two fragments of each
datagram survive the rate step. Both reassemblies therefore stall permanently, and no
further fragment of the first datagram will ever arrive.

Clause: RFC 1122 section 3.3.2. "The reassembly timeout MUST be a fixed value" and when
it expires "an ICMP Time Exceeded message SHOULD be sent to the source host, with code 1
(fragment reassembly time exceeded)". With fragmentTimeout at its 60 s default the first
datagram must be reaped at about t=61s -- driven by a timer, not by unrelated traffic --
and the resulting ICMP must carry code 1.

Findings IPV4-012 (the timeout is not timer driven) and IPV4-013 (wrong ICMP code).
Ipv4FragBuf::purgeStaleFragments() is called only from Ipv4::reassembleAndDeliver(), i.e.
only when some other fragment happens to arrive, so today the first datagram is reaped at
t=100s, 39 s late, and a receiver that stops getting fragments altogether never times out
at all. The call it makes is sendErrorMessage(..., ICMP_TIME_EXCEEDED, 0), i.e. code 0
("time to live exceeded in transit") instead of code 1.

%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%file: test.ned

import inet.networklayer.configurator.ipv4.Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;
import inet.node.inet.Router;
import inet.node.inet.StandardHost;
import ned.DatarateChannel;

network IcmpReassemblyNetwork
{
    types:
        channel fastline extends DatarateChannel {
            delay = 0.1us;
            datarate = 1Gbps;
        }
        channel slowline extends DatarateChannel {
            delay = 0.1us;
            datarate = 10Mbps;
        }
    submodules:
        configurator: Ipv4NetworkConfigurator;
        h1: StandardHost;
        r: Router;
        h2: StandardHost;
    connections:
        h1.ethg++ <--> fastline <--> r.ethg++;
        r.ethg++ <--> slowline <--> h2.ethg++;
}

%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
record-vector-results = false
record-scalar-results = false
network = IcmpReassemblyNetwork
ned-path = .;../../../../../src
sim-time-limit = 200s
cmdenv-express-mode = false
cmdenv-log-prefix = "t=%t %C: "

**.arp.typename = "GlobalArp"

# force fragmentation at h1
**.h1.eth[*].mac.mtu = 500B

# make the r --> h2 direction lose all but the first two fragments
**.r.eth[*].queue.typename = "DropTailQueue"
**.r.eth[*].queue.packetCapacity = 1

**.h1.numApps = 1
**.h1.app[0].typename = "UdpBasicApp"
**.h1.app[0].destAddresses = "h2"
**.h1.app[0].destPort = 1000
**.h1.app[0].messageLength = 3000B
**.h1.app[0].startTime = 1s
**.h1.app[0].sendInterval = 99s
**.h1.app[0].stopTime = 101s

**.h2.numApps = 1
**.h2.app[0].typename = "UdpSink"
**.h2.app[0].localPort = 1000

%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%# RFC 1122 3.3.2: the ICMP Time Exceeded must carry code 1, "fragment reassembly time exceeded"
%contains-regex: stdout
ICMP-error-#[0-9]+-type11-code1
%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%not-contains-regex: stdout
ICMP-error-#[0-9]+-type11-code0
%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%# the timeout must be driven by its own timer: the first datagram stalls at t~1s and
%# fragmentTimeout is 60s, so it must be reaped at t~61s and not when the second
%# datagram's first fragment happens to arrive at t~100s
%postrun-command: grep "ICMP_TIME_EXCEEDED" test.out | grep -oE "^t=[0-9.]+" | sort -u > timeexceeded_times.out || true
%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%contains-regex: timeexceeded_times.out
^t=6[01]\.
%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%not-contains-regex: timeexceeded_times.out
^t=100\.
%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%expected-failure: IPV4-012, IPV4-013 -- Ipv4FragBuf::purgeStaleFragments() runs only when another fragment arrives (from Ipv4::reassembleAndDeliver), so the stalled reassembly is reaped at t=100s instead of t=61s, and it calls sendErrorMessage(..., ICMP_TIME_EXCEEDED, 0), producing code 0 instead of the code 1 required by RFC 1122 3.3.2.
%#--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
